<section class="page-header"><div class="container"><div class="eyebrow">✈ Points and Miles Guide</div><h1>Using Points and Miles for Nonstop Flights: Best Programs by Route</h1><p>The best frequent flyer programs for nonstop award redemptions — by route, program, and how to actually find direct award space when searching.</p><div class="stat-strip"><div class="stat"><div class="stat-num">50k</div><div class="stat-label">Avios: LHR-JFK Business off-peak</div></div><div class="stat"><div class="stat-num">25k</div><div class="stat-label">Aeroplan: Economy to London</div></div><div class="stat"><div class="stat-num">89k</div><div class="stat-label">KrisFlyer: Business LHR-SIN</div></div></div></div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="container"><div class="prose">
<p>Award travel and nonstop flights are a natural combination. Using points for business class on a nonstop long-haul route is one of the genuinely excellent uses of frequent flyer miles — you are paying for both the premium cabin product and the elimination of connection risk simultaneously. The challenge is that award programs do not always make it easy to filter for nonstop-only availability, and many popular programs have moved toward dynamic pricing. This guide covers the programs with the best nonstop redemption value on major corridors, and how to search for direct award space specifically.</p>
<h2>British Airways Avios: Transatlantic Nonstop</h2>
<p>British Airways Executive Club uses Avios for redemptions on BA, Iberia, Aer Lingus, and American Airlines flights. For transatlantic nonstop routes, BA Avios is one of the most accessible programs for UK-based travelers.</p>
<p>Economy Avios on the BA nonstop LHR-JFK route runs from 13,000 off-peak to 26,000 peak each way. Business Class (Club World) runs from 50,000 off-peak to 100,000 in peak season each way. The off-peak Club World rate of 50,000 Avios is genuinely compelling value on a flat-bed transatlantic business class product, where cash fares routinely exceed $5,000 to $8,000 round-trip.</p>
<p>Important caveat: BA charges significant carrier-imposed surcharges on Avios redemptions on its own flights. On a Club World LHR-JFK award, you may pay $600 to $900 in fees on top of the Avios cost. To avoid these surcharges, use Avios to book on American Airlines — a oneworld partner — for the same transatlantic route. AA does not impose the same level of surcharges, so fees are typically under $100 rather than $800.</p>
<div class="tip-box"><p>💡 Avios transfer one-to-one between BA Executive Club, Iberia Plus, Aer Lingus AerClub, and Vueling Club. If BA is sold out of nonstop award space on your route, check Iberia's award calendar separately — they access the same Avios currency but sometimes show different availability windows for partner flights.</p></div>
<h2>Air Canada Aeroplan: Transatlantic and Pacific Nonstop</h2>
<p>Aeroplan underwent a significant overhaul in 2020 and is now one of the better structured programs for nonstop award redemptions globally. It uses a distance-based award chart rather than fully dynamic pricing, which means the headline rates are meaningful guides to actual costs.</p>
<p>Economy saver awards from Canada to Europe — including London, served nonstop from both Toronto and Vancouver — start from 25,000 Aeroplan points each way. That is among the better value transatlantic economy redemptions available to Canadian travelers. Business Class awards on nonstop transatlantic routes run 55,000 to 70,000 points each way depending on destination zone. Air Canada's Signature Class on long-haul routes features a lie-flat bed, making the Business Class award particularly compelling.</p>
<p>Aeroplan points can be earned on all Star Alliance partner flights — United, Lufthansa, Singapore Airlines, Swiss, Air New Zealand, and 35+ others — making it relatively straightforward to accumulate points for nonstop redemptions without flying Air Canada specifically. Aeroplan also partners with major hotel programs and credit cards for point earning.</p>
<h2>Singapore Airlines KrisFlyer: Premium Nonstop</h2>
<p>KrisFlyer is particularly valuable for Business Class redemptions on nonstop long-haul routes. The program uses a zone-based award chart and has maintained more consistent pricing than many competitors who have moved to dynamic models.</p>
<p>The Business Class (Saver) rate on London Heathrow to Singapore nonstop is 89,000 KrisFlyer miles each way. This covers a 13-hour flight on Singapore Airlines' award-winning Business Class — a forward-facing flat-bed with direct aisle access and consistently excellent catering. Cash fares on this route in Business run $5,000 to $8,000, making the 89,000-mile redemption outstanding value when you have the miles.</p>
<p>KrisFlyer Saver awards require finding available inventory — which Singapore Airlines releases in fixed quantities per flight. For peak travel dates and popular routes, Saver space goes quickly. Book six to eleven months in advance. For the ultra-long Singapore-JFK nonstop, Business Class Saver awards run approximately 119,000 miles each way, reflecting both the distance and the premium-only cabin on the A350-900ULR.</p>
<div class="info-box"><p>ℹ️ KrisFlyer miles are earned by flying Singapore Airlines and Star Alliance partners. Miles expire after 36 months without qualifying activity — make sure to maintain annual activity in the account to preserve your balance. Hotel transfers and credit card earning options are available to keep accounts active.</p></div>
<h2>Air France-KLM Flying Blue: Promo Awards</h2>
<p>Flying Blue is the loyalty program for Air France and KLM. Its standout feature is monthly Promo Awards — a selection of 12 to 15 routes at 25 to 50 percent off the standard award rate, published each month for departures two to three months out.</p>
<p>Many Promo Award routes are nonstop: Paris CDG to New York JFK, Amsterdam Schiphol to Los Angeles, Paris CDG to Montreal, Amsterdam to Cape Town, and similar corridors appear regularly. Economy promo awards on transatlantic nonstops have appeared at as few as 8,000 to 12,000 miles each way during strong promotion months — exceptional value. The limitation is you must be flexible enough to plan trips around the promotion calendar rather than the reverse.</p>
<h2>How to Search for Nonstop Award Space Specifically</h2>
<p>Most award booking tools do not offer a dedicated nonstop filter — they show award availability across all routings. Here is how to isolate direct award space on each major platform.</p>
<p><strong>British Airways Executive Club (ba.com):</strong> The results page shows each itinerary with its flight number. Single flight number results are nonstop. Multi-segment results show multiple flight numbers. A stops filter is also available in the right-hand filter panel on desktop.</p>
<p><strong>Air Canada Aeroplan (aeroplan.com):</strong> The award search has a Direct flights only checkbox under advanced filters. Check this to show only nonstop award availability on your route without connecting options appearing.</p>
<p><strong>KrisFlyer (singaporeair.com):</strong> Navigate to the Award Booking path. Results show by segment — nonstop itineraries display as a single flight number without a transfer airport listed. Filter by cabin class to see where direct seat availability is open.</p>
<p><strong>Flying Blue (airfrance.com or klm.com):</strong> Search for award flights and use the Nonstop filter in the left sidebar of results. Monthly Promo Awards are announced at flyingblue.com and include specific route and date details.</p>
<p><strong>Third-party tools (Point.me, Seats.aero):</strong> These aggregate award availability across multiple programs simultaneously and allow filtering for nonstop only, showing real-time availability across BA, Aeroplan, KrisFlyer, Flying Blue, and others. Both charge subscription fees but save significant time when searching across multiple programs for the same route. For frequent award travelers, the subscription cost is easily recovered in time savings.</p>
